From 811cae9c0e36f37f76fdb2469ae7e3f984f9005e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scratchydisk <stewart@amiwill.co.uk>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 08:19:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(opencode): rewrite the OpenCode 2 plugin entry against
 the v2 plugin API
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

src/entries/opencode.ts registered the v1 hook names (chat.message,
tool.execute.before, experimental.session.compacting,
experimental.chat.system.transform) and called app.tool(name, def). None of
those exist in OpenCode 2's plugin API, the v2 loader reads id/setup off the
default export, and the entry had no default export at all — the module could
not load under OpenCode 2.

Rewritten against the v2 plugin context:

  - tools: ctx.tool.transform(editor => editor.add(...)), codemode: false so
    the model can call the ctx_* tools directly (#1171)
  - tool hooks: ctx.tool.hook("execute.before" / "execute.after")
  - session hooks: ctx.session.hook("prompt" / "context" / "compaction")
  - events: ctx.event.subscribe(), with session.step.ended as the live source
    and message.part.updated kept as the fallback
  - cwd: ctx.location.directory
  - the module now default-exports { id, setup }

The system payload merges into the last SystemPart instead of adding one, so
system.length is unchanged and the provider prompt cache survives (#1085).

Compaction ownership and the destructive-tool policy are ported from
mksglu/context-mode#1194: in the default "own" mode the compaction hook
supplies event.result = { summary } so the host skips its own model
summarization call; "passthrough" leaves it to the host. ctx_purge and
ctx_upgrade are refused unless CONTEXT_MODE_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE=1. The summary
text is produced inside core (compactionSummary) so the entry still never
opens context.db.

The v1 names are absent from v2 core (opencode@1438073
packages/plugin/src/promise/session.ts:138-151, promise/tool.ts:38-64;
tickets/spike-hooks-verified.md:47). impl/12 §4 already requires {id, setup}
and the ctx.tool.transform/editor.add pattern, so this entry now matches its
own spec.

tests/v2/plugins/opencode.test.mjs covers the v2 surface (tool registration,
hook names, cleanup), the compaction ownership modes, and the destructive-tool
policy.
